<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06KK">Remark <span data-tag="06KK">9.4.7.14</span>.</a> Following the convention of Remark <a data-tag="03PU" href="/tag/03PU">4.9.0.4</a>, an $\infty $-category is essentially small if it is essentially $ \Omega $-small, where $ \Omega $ is some fixed inaccessible cardinal. An accessible $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ need not satisfy this condition. However, it follows from Corollary <a data-tag="06FZ" href="/tag/06FZ">9.3.3.2</a> that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is locally $ \Omega $-small and essentially $ \Omega ^{+}$-small. Moreover, $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ admits small $\kappa $-filtered colimits for some small regular cardinal $\kappa $ (and therefore for every small regular cardinal $\lambda \geq \kappa $). In particular, $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is idempotent-complete (Example <a data-tag="0617" href="/tag/0617">9.1.1.10</a>). </p>
